<!-- cell: salient-concept id="65c61d18-63a6-4ede-9308-d12bc65e51e7" title="competing for love of a woman" -->

Rivalry between two men - Francis Tomson and Jonathan Childes - who want the affection of the same woman - Jane Bolton. Winning her becomes a test of worth as much as an expression of love. Here the contest is less about grand gestures than about character: the woman measures each suitor by how he stands up to wrong and by whether he is his own man or still living in his father's shadow.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="23a39643-b4be-4adf-9a77-f6c4e6fac299" title="fighting the injustice - being righteous but not idealistic" -->

Francis Tomson opposes the wrong done to others while accepting that the world will not be made pure, choosing practical victories, compromise and sometimes uncomfortable means over moral perfection. The story shows this as a mature kind of courage that sets the son apart from both the cynics and the dreamers around him, though his father reads it as weakness or folly and his rival may exploit it.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="c58b0777-c2f7-474b-bb40-a5ea785775ff" title="generational conflict, father not approving of the son" -->

The clash between Francis Tomson and his father, Benedict Tomson. Father's expectations differ those from Francis's, who chooses different values and a different path. Francis is left craving the father's approval that is withheld. The story shows the father's disapproval as both a wound and a whetstone: it pushes the son to prove himself in his fight against injustice and in his pursuit of the woman, until he must decide whether he needs his father's blessing at all.

<!-- cell: plot id="6a3c93ad-f300-4d20-84a1-7e67419f1b11" title="competing for love of a woman" edges="9843c34f-6b6a-44f0-8b63-4654f7faec51:47aea612-8359-4ec5-adac-5823fcacfd1c>45a6001c-e6d7-41ea-a1c7-a8c2dfc4b4d6, b61f6c23-25f0-4473-a7f6-e43f84d1c158:45a6001c-e6d7-41ea-a1c7-a8c2dfc4b4d6>b004bcc1-5b19-426a-a82a-db441a8102a2, cd62b00b-22a6-46fe-acab-cba1b7d4391c:b004bcc1-5b19-426a-a82a-db441a8102a2>0ef068d4-ac7f-41de-9470-465f2f859a0a, c9a14e98-0ce0-4545-9287-dfd85c38740d:0ef068d4-ac7f-41de-9470-465f2f859a0a>48c4f5a8-a0b2-409f-82f7-48f757808bb0, 0c4d1796-00e4-48c6-9e5d-f61e36b113b5:48c4f5a8-a0b2-409f-82f7-48f757808bb0>afc45fd6-6b72-4d5f-874f-c80bfb87120d, de6c0c10-bfce-4a0a-a341-98b321c1958e:afc45fd6-6b72-4d5f-874f-c80bfb87120d>95dce314-75a1-47eb-af3f-c88646b9c16a, 69199950-bb5b-4865-b4bf-b94422d5ed2f:95dce314-75a1-47eb-af3f-c88646b9c16a>f7f94719-e210-406a-84bb-fbc298d8061e, 5feb176c-c75a-45b4-9867-7c06a0d0ec8f:f7f94719-e210-406a-84bb-fbc298d8061e>272bb095-b709-4d6c-9b49-b234fd1f2bc2, 684065b1-e871-4314-a51c-91bac6d51744:272bb095-b709-4d6c-9b49-b234fd1f2bc2>108746f7-a293-435d-9e14-28f0be0846f9, 56c0d69b-541c-46b5-80c5-681f1479eed5:108746f7-a293-435d-9e14-28f0be0846f9>522d45de-97d1-4636-8c30-94795384fd9a, 764808ca-87b1-469d-92de-7528929fd440:522d45de-97d1-4636-8c30-94795384fd9a>4320b292-aae4-49c1-a0d5-56780204c018" concepts="65c61d18-63a6-4ede-9308-d12bc65e51e7" -->

<ol>
<li id="47aea612-8359-4ec5-adac-5823fcacfd1c" timestamp="1.0" location="England, Hollowmere village, the green at the harvest fair" words="513-1052">Francis Tomson and Jonathan Childes both ask to walk Jane Bolton home. Just then old Squire Tomson's bailiff turns the widow Marsh out of her cottage, and Jane watches as Francis stays silent at his father's side while Jonathan only shouts and does nothing.</li>
<li id="45a6001c-e6d7-41ea-a1c7-a8c2dfc4b4d6" timestamp="2" location="England, Hollowmere, Weavers' Row, the evicted Ashby cottage doorstep at dusk" words="0-471">Jane brings bread and a blanket to the widow Clara Ashby, who sits among her bundled belongings, just as Francis arrives. Stung by his father's refusal and set on proving his choice was no betrayal, he has come for evidence. Clara shows them the eviction records she has kept in secret, and they reveal that Squire Benedict Tomson took the weavers' cottages only to widen his own pasture. Hearing this hardens Jane's resolve to judge Francis and Jonathan by whether either will set the wrong right. Francis then files the weavers' petition with the magistrates' court, knowing it will come before his father's own bench.</li>
<li id="b004bcc1-5b19-426a-a82a-db441a8102a2" timestamp="3" location="England, Hollowmere village, the lane outside the Bolton cottage" words="1081-1685">Francis and Jonathan each come calling with a harvest gift just as Francis's father, having heard the spreading word of his son's quiet payments, rides up and scorns them before everyone as a soft fool's charity. Francis gives up on changing his father's mind but still asks him for a modest sick-pay fund, and when his father refuses outright he resolves to win it through the town council instead. Jane then turns both suitors away at the gate. She tells Francis that a man who hides behind his father's coat is no man to lean on, and Jonathan that noise is not courage, and she will walk with neither until one of them makes the widow Marsh's wrong right.</li>
<li id="0ef068d4-ac7f-41de-9470-465f2f859a0a" timestamp="4.0" location="England, Hollowmere village, the widow Marsh's cottage" words="1723-2194">Spurred by Jane's challenge, both suitors come to the widow Marsh's eviction, but Francis stands mute at his father's side as the bailiff carries out her goods and Jonathan only shouts threats from the crowd, so Jane, watching, walks home alone and refuses either man's arm.</li>
<li id="48c4f5a8-a0b2-409f-82f7-48f757808bb0" timestamp="5" location="England, Hollowmere village, the lane by the Bolton farm gate" words="2216-2688">When both suitors come calling the next morning, Jane Bolton turns them away at the gate. She tells Jonathan that shouting from a crowd is not courage, and tells Francis she will not marry a man who stands silent behind his father, declaring she will walk with neither until one proves himself his own man by righting the wrong done to the widow Marsh. When she presses Francis that he owes his father nothing, he keeps glancing down the lane where Benedict never appears and admits he still wants his father's approval. That night he rides out to ask Benedict's blessing before he will take up her challenge and seek her hand.</li>
<li id="afc45fd6-6b72-4d5f-874f-c80bfb87120d" timestamp="6" location="England, Hollowmere village, the manor court in the church hall" words="2737-3314">Stung that Jane has refused to walk with either suitor since the eviction, Francis stands before the assembled villagers and overrules his father, ordering the bailiff to return the widow Marsh's cottage. With more sick weavers coming to him than his wages can cover, he then turns to Edmund Crane on the front bench and asks for his council votes on a sick-pay fund, knowing his father would never allow it. He listens without protest as Crane names his price, support for the canal scheme, and says he will consider it, while Jonathan sits in the back still boasting of what he would have done.</li>
<li id="95dce314-75a1-47eb-af3f-c88646b9c16a" timestamp="6.0" location="England, Hollowmere village, the Tomson manor hall" words="3365-3957">Furious at being overruled before the village, Squire Tomson threatens to disinherit Francis unless he reverses the ruling, but Francis walks out to fetch the widow Marsh's furniture from the bailiff's barn himself, while Jonathan stops Jane at the well to boast he would have done it sooner, so Jane goes to the widow's lane to see which man's deeds match his words.</li>
<li id="f7f94719-e210-406a-84bb-fbc298d8061e" timestamp="8" location="England, Hollowmere village, the bailiff's barn" words="3983-4556">When the bailiff refuses to release the widow Marsh's furniture without the squire's word, Francis pays the disputed debt from his own purse and begins loading the cart with his own hands. Jonathan, who followed Jane's path, slips away the moment the squire's horse is heard on the road. The rider is Benedict, come from London with Francis's letter in his coat. He admits he read it twice and lingered over the line about the empty seat in the chapel, but he still counts the weavers' case a betrayal of his own class and Clara's family beneath the Tomson name. He swallows the warmer words he had half-prepared and gives his son three cold lines refusing to come to the wedding at Ashbury. Francis finishes loading in silence and drives the cart alone toward the widow's lane, with his father riding hard behind him.</li>
<li id="272bb095-b709-4d6c-9b49-b234fd1f2bc2" timestamp="9" location="England, Hollowmere village, the lane outside the widow Marsh's cottage" words="4598-5255">While Jane watches, Francis carries the widow's furniture back inside and refuses his furious father's order to undo the court ruling. When Crane, who has come along with his father, presses the canal scheme, Francis agrees to back it on one condition: that its route spare the weavers' cottages on Mill Lane. He shakes on the deal while Crane mocks his scruples, because a fund won by bargaining feeds people and a refusal on principle feeds no one. All the while Jonathan only talks about what he would have done, and Jane, seeing Francis stand as his own man at last, agrees to walk with him again.</li>
<li id="108746f7-a293-435d-9e14-28f0be0846f9" timestamp="8.0" location="England, Hollowmere village, the churchyard after Sunday service" words="5295-5696">On their walk Jonathan stops Jane and Francis and boasts again of how he would have faced down old Tomson. Jane asks why he only ever speaks of it and never acts. Francis says nothing for himself but asks Jane to come with him to the widow's cottage, where he has spent the week mending the roof his father let rot.</li>
<li id="522d45de-97d1-4636-8c30-94795384fd9a" timestamp="11" location="England, Hollowmere village, the widow Marsh's cottage" words="5726-6134">When old Benedict Tomson arrives with his bailiff to evict the widow, Francis stands in the doorway before Jane and the gathered villagers, among them old Tobias, the eldest of the weavers he defended in court. He tears up the eviction notice and declares the cottage hers for life. He waits a moment for a word from his father that never comes, then hands the torn pieces to Tobias, choosing the people he fought for over the blessing Benedict still withholds, while Jonathan, who came along to watch, hangs back in silence.</li>
<li id="4320b292-aae4-49c1-a0d5-56780204c018" timestamp="12" location="England, Harrowgate, town hall, council chamber" words="6171-6825">Francis wins the vote for a sick-pay fund for the mill workers and the return of the widow Marsh's Hollowmere cottage by trading his support for his rival Edmund Crane's canal scheme, openly defying his father, who calls it a coward's bargain. Francis accepts that Crane will profit because forty families will eat this winter. Afterward Jane, who saw him finally stand as his own man against wrong, accepts his proposal there in the chamber. Jonathan, who never stopped boasting of what he would have done, wishes them well and admits he lost her to a better man.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: plot id="1ffb274b-5784-41d4-9bef-027b5df7eff0" title="fighting the injustice - being righteous but not idealistic" edges="bd671341-b7f1-4866-92e3-e6811098dc2f:dbd5543c-208a-443c-b0f2-d030c954361b>c9c271fe-5f60-4336-bc01-10c9eb320180, 0c9e7116-804c-4a91-b9ae-2f8b6ec0498a:c9c271fe-5f60-4336-bc01-10c9eb320180>5bcd2f1e-77b7-47c1-b411-1715da5625a1, 50ecdbcc-47e3-4efa-b3bd-1ff42e84f9d7:5bcd2f1e-77b7-47c1-b411-1715da5625a1>3838d6a1-4f9d-4949-837a-679ab2841cb1, c578f2cb-4e64-4fa1-8ac2-ad82031aa1d4:3838d6a1-4f9d-4949-837a-679ab2841cb1>4f8ca34e-223e-4e43-99ce-2ce4c0c0801c, 9788f0f5-d9a6-40ca-8f81-d6cc7cf2e0c7:4f8ca34e-223e-4e43-99ce-2ce4c0c0801c>60152554-e9a4-4a8a-aec4-6cc91efa0587, 3443d3f0-aede-42cf-8909-dba8c7ebb7ea:60152554-e9a4-4a8a-aec4-6cc91efa0587>506958d0-7248-477b-88e8-f61e6f502bf0, 7665e428-0568-4fdb-9244-bd0e1e2ded4b:506958d0-7248-477b-88e8-f61e6f502bf0>58aaa3d1-b3e0-4aef-9b46-734ae4b4a209, 72671dbb-3802-4640-a46b-3d32635a131b:58aaa3d1-b3e0-4aef-9b46-734ae4b4a209>e2449759-3af2-4577-935c-a879cd06323e, 0ae89e6a-2887-4cf0-bcbd-f686edaaca29:e2449759-3af2-4577-935c-a879cd06323e>cf96fd93-ca0c-4a00-90da-ed12cf104122, bdfdb6af-1a1c-41be-a38a-5acc8ef362b0:cf96fd93-ca0c-4a00-90da-ed12cf104122>f341ce5d-8cc6-4db7-85e3-24a15becd0db, e30aaf31-86c8-47cc-a2d0-69447f142ab8:f341ce5d-8cc6-4db7-85e3-24a15becd0db>a7caf9e6-5e07-42b0-a07a-fa32b3f5773e" concepts="23a39643-b4be-4adf-9a77-f6c4e6fac299" -->

<ol>
<li id="dbd5543c-208a-443c-b0f2-d030c954361b" timestamp="1" location="England, Harrowgate, Tomson mill, weaving floor" words="6826-7304">Francis Tomson watches the foreman dock a fevered weaver a week's pay while his father Benedict, who built the mill and sits as the town's magistrate, looks on approvingly; knowing Benedict will back the foreman, Francis does not shout but quietly presses the lost wages from his own pay into her hand, then tells his father he will not take his seat at the mill and will instead defend the evicted weavers in court, and Benedict calls the choice a betrayal and refuses him his blessing.</li>
<li id="c9c271fe-5f60-4336-bc01-10c9eb320180" timestamp="2.4" location="Harrowgate, Red Lion tavern, back room" words="7351-7897">More sick weavers come to Francis for their docked wages, and when their firebrand spokesman urges him to back a walkout, he refuses because it would only get them all sacked, offering instead to keep covering their losses and press for a proper sick fund, a bargain the grateful weavers boast about loudly enough to carry through the town.</li>
<li id="5bcd2f1e-77b7-47c1-b411-1715da5625a1" timestamp="3" location="England, Hollowmere village, the lane outside the Bolton cottage" words="1081-1685">Francis and Jonathan each come calling with a harvest gift just as Francis's father, having heard the spreading word of his son's quiet payments, rides up and scorns them before everyone as a soft fool's charity. Francis gives up on changing his father's mind but still asks him for a modest sick-pay fund, and when his father refuses outright he resolves to win it through the town council instead. Jane then turns both suitors away at the gate. She tells Francis that a man who hides behind his father's coat is no man to lean on, and Jonathan that noise is not courage, and she will walk with neither until one of them makes the widow Marsh's wrong right.</li>
<li id="3838d6a1-4f9d-4949-837a-679ab2841cb1" timestamp="4" location="England, Harrowgate, town hall, council chamber" words="7930-8591">As fever spreads through the weavers' rows, Francis puts a sick-pay motion to the council and trims it to a bare fund despite the reformer Hale scorning anything short of a ten-hour bill. When his father Benedict rises for the mill owners and calls the weavers' claims idle grievances, Francis answers him with Clara Ashby's eviction records. The motion carries only because Francis accepts Crane's bloc's votes. Benedict leaves the chamber without a word to his son, and Francis, stung, resolves to go home and ask his blessing to marry Clara anyway.</li>
<li id="4f8ca34e-223e-4e43-99ce-2ce4c0c0801c" timestamp="5.1" location="Harrowgate, the weavers' rows, the Dobbs cottage" words="8631-9129">With the motion lost and fever spreading, Francis pays a sick weaver's rent and doctor from his own wages, shrugging off Hale's sneer that charity only props up the mills and his father's warning that it is soft-headed folly. Word of his help spreads through the rows and more stricken families begin knocking at his door.</li>
<li id="60152554-e9a4-4a8a-aec4-6cc91efa0587" timestamp="6" location="England, Hollowmere village, the manor court in the church hall" words="2737-3314">Stung that Jane has refused to walk with either suitor since the eviction, Francis stands before the assembled villagers and overrules his father, ordering the bailiff to return the widow Marsh's cottage. With more sick weavers coming to him than his wages can cover, he then turns to Edmund Crane on the front bench and asks for his council votes on a sick-pay fund, knowing his father would never allow it. He listens without protest as Crane names his price, support for the canal scheme, and says he will consider it, while Jonathan sits in the back still boasting of what he would have done.</li>
<li id="506958d0-7248-477b-88e8-f61e6f502bf0" timestamp="7" location="England, Harrowgate, Mill Lane, a sick weaver's cottage" words="9153-9863">Sitting up with a fevered weaver, Francis finds canal survey stakes driven through the Mill Lane gardens, and when the chapel preacher urges him to denounce Crane and refuse any deal, he decides that refusing would save no cottages and feed no one, so he resolves to bargain over the canal's route instead. Then, still unable to let his father's refusal stand, he spends the rest of the night at the bedside drafting a long letter to Benedict. In it he sets out how the weavers' case has been argued honorably, even in compromise, and how Clara's family has sheltered him. He begs his father to come to the wedding and tells him a seat is left for him in the chapel, then sends the letter off at dawn, hoping Benedict will finally reply with his blessing.</li>
<li id="58aaa3d1-b3e0-4aef-9b46-734ae4b4a209" timestamp="6.8" location="Harrowgate, Tomson house, father's study" words="9899-10343">Francis spreads a borrowed survey map he bought from Crane's surveyor's clerk with a quiet bribe and traces a costlier route around Mill Lane through the Tomson water meadow, and when his father calls it a weakling's folly to give up family land and dirty his hands with a canal he despises, Francis answers that clean hands have never kept a roof over anyone, and he resolves to carry the alternate route to Crane as his price.</li>
<li id="e2449759-3af2-4577-935c-a879cd06323e" timestamp="9" location="England, Hollowmere village, the lane outside the widow Marsh's cottage" words="4598-5255">While Jane watches, Francis carries the widow's furniture back inside and refuses his furious father's order to undo the court ruling. When Crane, who has come along with his father, presses the canal scheme, Francis agrees to back it on one condition: that its route spare the weavers' cottages on Mill Lane. He shakes on the deal while Crane mocks his scruples, because a fund won by bargaining feeds people and a refusal on principle feeds no one. All the while Jonathan only talks about what he would have done, and Jane, seeing Francis stand as his own man at last, agrees to walk with him again.</li>
<li id="cf96fd93-ca0c-4a00-90da-ed12cf104122" timestamp="10" location="England, Ashbury village, chapel" words="10366-10868">On the wedding morning, with the front pew still kept empty for Benedict, Francis learns at the chapel door that Crane's surveyors have staked the canal route through the weavers' cottages after all. Rather than break the deal and lose the fund, he takes Crane aside in the porch and warns him he will withhold his vote unless the line is moved, and he lets Crane have the common meadow instead, because saving the homes and the fund matters more than winning every point. He glances at the door once more for his father as the vows begin, but when the weavers whose homes he has just saved fill the benches behind Clara, he turns from the door and speaks his vows to her without waiting any longer.</li>
<li id="f341ce5d-8cc6-4db7-85e3-24a15becd0db" timestamp="9.1" location="Harrowgate, Tomson house, father's study" words="10918-11451">After Crane moves the canal line off the cottages, Francis's father learns he gave up the common meadow and calls him a coward. Francis still refuses to break with Crane, and gets Crane's written promise of grazing money for the commoners, because a paid loss is better than a noble defeat that feeds no one.</li>
<li id="a7caf9e6-5e07-42b0-a07a-fa32b3f5773e" timestamp="12" location="England, Harrowgate, town hall, council chamber" words="6171-6825">Francis wins the vote for a sick-pay fund for the mill workers and the return of the widow Marsh's Hollowmere cottage by trading his support for his rival Edmund Crane's canal scheme, openly defying his father, who calls it a coward's bargain. Francis accepts that Crane will profit because forty families will eat this winter. Afterward Jane, who saw him finally stand as his own man against wrong, accepts his proposal there in the chamber. Jonathan, who never stopped boasting of what he would have done, wishes them well and admits he lost her to a better man.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: plot id="78edbb72-2468-4a3c-a4f7-06b02df3e240" title="generational conflict, father not approving of the son" edges="7a089585-320f-401d-9c42-c2cc38a7aa14:b83e499f-195c-45f7-b558-08d8bcf65c9e>1b565867-2226-4e25-aba3-0c43197492ba, 788c1736-f04c-43ef-a7bc-389afce03470:1b565867-2226-4e25-aba3-0c43197492ba>278bf0a2-4a4f-4726-8b4e-e2009f493d33, f4cad600-0fca-4584-9b00-765f2c784d22:278bf0a2-4a4f-4726-8b4e-e2009f493d33>7608351d-663b-4443-850f-283a5e60b1f1, 2adabf52-4a84-47b8-a8ad-e00abbf832cb:7608351d-663b-4443-850f-283a5e60b1f1>30c581f9-8eaf-4490-9c11-7e06b0b7d67a, 38af73ce-3867-4bb9-befb-a89c4f0a244e:30c581f9-8eaf-4490-9c11-7e06b0b7d67a>9befbe24-13b4-4246-9599-797a30a1fbd2, 6778afab-8b6d-4a23-9954-9eb738d02011:9befbe24-13b4-4246-9599-797a30a1fbd2>0a900634-6216-4086-87b0-f8a3bc4fb140, f0e89a44-6f54-426a-9e43-cdbb15ea94f2:0a900634-6216-4086-87b0-f8a3bc4fb140>f2fb17fa-898e-4148-a041-ddeb400dc2a8, 71a466cd-a6d0-47d2-9dd6-224815985a78:f2fb17fa-898e-4148-a041-ddeb400dc2a8>15e30beb-3a56-42e5-8ad2-034232537b69, 81a89b65-7856-4455-a934-2c8a3a60084a:15e30beb-3a56-42e5-8ad2-034232537b69>4595fcb5-511b-419c-8517-ff20e95a8dba, b5b153f0-1f35-43d6-ab6d-2dbde37d14b0:4595fcb5-511b-419c-8517-ff20e95a8dba>da5344a4-5806-4729-9981-8bb2d5011da2, ec5dcef5-dd1d-4c8d-ba81-e0faaee00d30:da5344a4-5806-4729-9981-8bb2d5011da2>1f56f445-55de-4ac6-915c-0a184cfbd690" concepts="c58b0777-c2f7-474b-bb40-a5ea785775ff" -->

<ol>
<li id="b83e499f-195c-45f7-b558-08d8bcf65c9e" timestamp="1" location="England, Harrowgate, Tomson mill, weaving floor" words="6826-7304">Francis Tomson watches the foreman dock a fevered weaver a week's pay while his father Benedict, who built the mill and sits as the town's magistrate, looks on approvingly; knowing Benedict will back the foreman, Francis does not shout but quietly presses the lost wages from his own pay into her hand, then tells his father he will not take his seat at the mill and will instead defend the evicted weavers in court, and Benedict calls the choice a betrayal and refuses him his blessing.</li>
<li id="1b565867-2226-4e25-aba3-0c43197492ba" timestamp="2" location="England, Hollowmere, Weavers' Row, the evicted Ashby cottage doorstep at dusk" words="0-471">Jane brings bread and a blanket to the widow Clara Ashby, who sits among her bundled belongings, just as Francis arrives. Stung by his father's refusal and set on proving his choice was no betrayal, he has come for evidence. Clara shows them the eviction records she has kept in secret, and they reveal that Squire Benedict Tomson took the weavers' cottages only to widen his own pasture. Hearing this hardens Jane's resolve to judge Francis and Jonathan by whether either will set the wrong right. Francis then files the weavers' petition with the magistrates' court, knowing it will come before his father's own bench.</li>
<li id="278bf0a2-4a4f-4726-8b4e-e2009f493d33" timestamp="4.0" location="England, Hallowmere town, the magistrates' court" words="11476-11956">When Benedict dismisses the weavers' petition from the bench without looking at his son, Francis vows to carry the case to the county assizes to prove him wrong, and Clara Ashby, the weaver's daughter who kept the eviction records, agrees to testify and later accepts his proposal on the courthouse steps.</li>
<li id="7608351d-663b-4443-850f-283a5e60b1f1" timestamp="4" location="England, Harrowgate, town hall, council chamber" words="7930-8591">As fever spreads through the weavers' rows, Francis puts a sick-pay motion to the council and trims it to a bare fund despite the reformer Hale scorning anything short of a ten-hour bill. When his father Benedict rises for the mill owners and calls the weavers' claims idle grievances, Francis answers him with Clara Ashby's eviction records. The motion carries only because Francis accepts Crane's bloc's votes. Benedict leaves the chamber without a word to his son, and Francis, stung, resolves to go home and ask his blessing to marry Clara anyway.</li>
<li id="30c581f9-8eaf-4490-9c11-7e06b0b7d67a" timestamp="5" location="England, Hollowmere village, the lane by the Bolton farm gate" words="2216-2688">When both suitors come calling the next morning, Jane Bolton turns them away at the gate. She tells Jonathan that shouting from a crowd is not courage, and tells Francis she will not marry a man who stands silent behind his father, declaring she will walk with neither until one proves himself his own man by righting the wrong done to the widow Marsh. When she presses Francis that he owes his father nothing, he keeps glancing down the lane where Benedict never appears and admits he still wants his father's approval. That night he rides out to ask Benedict's blessing before he will take up her challenge and seek her hand.</li>
<li id="9befbe24-13b4-4246-9599-797a30a1fbd2" timestamp="7.0" location="England, Hallowmere estate, Benedict Tomson's study" words="11972-12464">Fresh from winning the weavers' case at the county assizes, Francis returns to ask his father's blessing to marry Clara Ashby, hoping the victory will finally earn his respect, but Benedict calls the verdict a humiliation of the mill and the match a disgrace to the family, and says he will not attend the wedding.</li>
<li id="0a900634-6216-4086-87b0-f8a3bc4fb140" timestamp="7" location="England, Harrowgate, Mill Lane, a sick weaver's cottage" words="9153-9863">Sitting up with a fevered weaver, Francis finds canal survey stakes driven through the Mill Lane gardens, and when the chapel preacher urges him to denounce Crane and refuse any deal, he decides that refusing would save no cottages and feed no one, so he resolves to bargain over the canal's route instead. Then, still unable to let his father's refusal stand, he spends the rest of the night at the bedside drafting a long letter to Benedict. In it he sets out how the weavers' case has been argued honorably, even in compromise, and how Clara's family has sheltered him. He begs his father to come to the wedding and tells him a seat is left for him in the chapel, then sends the letter off at dawn, hoping Benedict will finally reply with his blessing.</li>
<li id="f2fb17fa-898e-4148-a041-ddeb400dc2a8" timestamp="8" location="England, Hollowmere village, the bailiff's barn" words="3983-4556">When the bailiff refuses to release the widow Marsh's furniture without the squire's word, Francis pays the disputed debt from his own purse and begins loading the cart with his own hands. Jonathan, who followed Jane's path, slips away the moment the squire's horse is heard on the road. The rider is Benedict, come from London with Francis's letter in his coat. He admits he read it twice and lingered over the line about the empty seat in the chapel, but he still counts the weavers' case a betrayal of his own class and Clara's family beneath the Tomson name. He swallows the warmer words he had half-prepared and gives his son three cold lines refusing to come to the wedding at Ashbury. Francis finishes loading in silence and drives the cart alone toward the widow's lane, with his father riding hard behind him.</li>
<li id="15e30beb-3a56-42e5-8ad2-034232537b69" timestamp="8.0" location="England, Ashbury village, the Ashbys' cottage" words="12488-12990">On the eve of the wedding Francis gets a curt letter in his father's hand repeating that he will not come, and he nearly asks Clara to delay the vows in hope Benedict will relent, until her father reminds him that the weavers who are back in their homes are the only verdict on his work that matters, and Francis resolves to marry her the next morning as planned.</li>
<li id="4595fcb5-511b-419c-8517-ff20e95a8dba" timestamp="10" location="England, Ashbury village, chapel" words="10366-10868">On the wedding morning, with the front pew still kept empty for Benedict, Francis learns at the chapel door that Crane's surveyors have staked the canal route through the weavers' cottages after all. Rather than break the deal and lose the fund, he takes Crane aside in the porch and warns him he will withhold his vote unless the line is moved, and he lets Crane have the common meadow instead, because saving the homes and the fund matters more than winning every point. He glances at the door once more for his father as the vows begin, but when the weavers whose homes he has just saved fill the benches behind Clara, he turns from the door and speaks his vows to her without waiting any longer.</li>
<li id="da5344a4-5806-4729-9981-8bb2d5011da2" timestamp="11" location="England, Hollowmere village, the widow Marsh's cottage" words="5726-6134">When old Benedict Tomson arrives with his bailiff to evict the widow, Francis stands in the doorway before Jane and the gathered villagers, among them old Tobias, the eldest of the weavers he defended in court. He tears up the eviction notice and declares the cottage hers for life. He waits a moment for a word from his father that never comes, then hands the torn pieces to Tobias, choosing the people he fought for over the blessing Benedict still withholds, while Jonathan, who came along to watch, hangs back in silence.</li>
<li id="1f56f445-55de-4ac6-915c-0a184cfbd690" timestamp="9.0" location="England, Ashbury village, chapel steps" words="13016-13457">Francis walks out of the chapel married to Clara Ashby, the weaver's daughter, with the weavers' homes restored by his victory in court, and sees his father's empty place in the pews. He stops waiting for Benedict's approval and goes on without it.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="9af7fb40-6dc2-446c-9b96-aeb815b97e19" -->

Dusk had come down grey over Weavers' Row, and the shuttered cottages stood in a line like teeth with the nerve pulled out. On the doorstep of the last of them sat Clara Ashby, wrapped in a shawl too thin for October, with her life tied up in three bundles of sacking and a loom-frame lashed with cord. Jane came up the lane with a loaf under her arm and a folded blanket over the other, and she had only just knelt to tuck the wool about the widow's knees when boots sounded on the cobbles. Francis was walking fast, hatless, his jaw set hard. He stopped short at the sight of Jane, and something passed over his face that might have been shame or might have been relief.

"I need proof," he said, before anyone could greet him. "Not talk in the alehouse. Proof. My father says there was cause, and that I've no business siding against my own blood. I mean to show him I've betrayed nothing but a lie."

Clara looked at him a long moment, then reached into the nearest bundle and drew out a packet bound in oilcloth. Inside, in a careful, cramped hand, were the notices served on every household in the Row, each one copied out, dated, and initialed by the bailiff who had brought it. Beneath them lay a surveyor's sketch she had no right to possess, and on it the cottages were marked out in faint pencil, and over them, in firm ink, ran the new boundary of Squire Benedict Tomson's pasture. "Rents were paid," Clara said quietly. "Every one. He wanted the grass, not the money." Jane read the sketch twice by the last of the light, and when she lifted her eyes they had gone very still. She said nothing to Francis, but she was thinking, coldly and clearly, that soft words from him or from Jonathan Pell would weigh nothing with her now. She would watch which of them, if either, put this right, and judge them by that alone.

Francis took the packet as if it were hot iron. He sat down on the step beside the widow, borrowed Jane's pencil, and wrote the weavers' petition out against his knee, names and dates and the squire's own boundary set down plainly. Then he rose and went. The magistrates' clerk was turning his key in the lock when Francis reached the court in the market square, and he opened again only grudgingly. Francis laid the papers on the counter and watched them entered in the ledger under the next sitting of the bench, the bench at which his father presided. The clerk blotted the line, glanced at the name of the petitioner's advocate, and looked up sharply. Francis held his gaze, said good night, and walked out into the dark.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="b61f6c23-25f0-4473-a7f6-e43f84d1c158">Francis and Jonathan Pell each set out with a harvest gift for the lane outside the Bolton cottage.</li>
<li edge="788c1736-f04c-43ef-a7bc-389afce03470">Francis went up to the magistrates' court on the morning the bench sat to hear the weavers' petition.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="dcb45121-e22b-4ee4-9fa8-3420b686783d" -->

The green at Hollowmere had been trodden to bare earth by late afternoon, and the smell of cider and crushed apples hung over it like weather. Trestles sagged under marrows and pies; a fiddler sawed away beneath the oak, and children ran shrieking between the stalls with ribbons won at the shooting butts. Jane Bolton stood near the cake table in her best blue stuff gown, her mother's shawl about her shoulders, and watched the sun go copper behind the church tower.

Francis Tomson reached her first. He wore a good brown coat that had been made for him in Salisbury, and he took off his hat before he spoke, which no other young man on the green had thought to do. He asked, very civilly, whether he might see her home when the dancing was done. He had scarcely finished when Jonathan Childes came shouldering through the crowd, red from the wrestling and still in his shirtsleeves, and said loudly that he had meant to ask her that very thing and had only been waiting till the Squire's son had done talking. The two young men looked at one another. Jane opened her mouth to answer them both and found she had no answer ready.

A commotion at the far side of the green spared her. A cart had drawn up before the low thatched cottage at the corner of Mill Lane, and Squire Tomson's bailiff, a thick-necked man named Hobbs, stood in the doorway with two labourers behind him. They were carrying out the widow Marsh's things: a three-legged stool, a bundle of bedding, a clock with its case cracked, a cage with a linnet in it. The widow herself came after, gripping the doorpost with both hands until Hobbs prised her fingers away, and then she sat down in the road among her goods with her apron over her face. The fiddler stopped. Across the green the fair went quiet, one stall after another.

Old Squire Tomson sat his grey mare at the edge of the crowd, and Francis had gone to stand at his stirrup without a word, as if called. Jane watched him there. He looked at the widow, and once at his father, and then down at the turf, and his hands stayed folded behind his back. Jonathan had run halfway across the green and stopped in the open, and from there he bellowed that it was a black shame, that the woman had paid her rent forty years, that Hobbs was a dog and the Squire no better. His voice carried to every corner of Hollowmere. But he came no nearer to the cart, and when Hobbs turned his heavy face toward him, Jonathan went on shouting from where he stood.

The labourers set the last of the furniture in the road and nailed a board across the cottage door. The linnet sang once in its cage. Jane stood very still by the cake table, the shawl gathered tight at her throat, and looked from the young man who had said nothing to the young man who had said everything, while the widow Marsh wept in the dust of Mill Lane and the shadow of the church tower crept out across the green.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="9843c34f-6b6a-44f0-8b63-4654f7faec51">As dusk fell, Jane carried bread and a blanket to the widow Clara Ashby, sitting among her bundles on her doorstep in Weavers' Row.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="53e37340-4e62-4f4b-ad75-5fec66268c94" -->

The lane outside the Bolton cottage was rutted and dry, and the hedges on either side hung heavy with blackberries gone soft in the September heat. Francis came first, a basket of russet apples on his arm, the cloth over them folded with a care that suggested he had folded it more than once. Jonathan came hard behind him from the other end of the lane, carrying a sheaf of wheat bound with a red ribbon. He had plainly meant to be seen carrying it. The two men stopped a few yards apart before the Boltons' gate and regarded each other without a word. Then hooves sounded on the lane, and Mr. Ashworth rode up on his grey mare, reined in so close that the apples trembled in their basket, and looked down at his son.

"So it's true," he said, loud enough for the woman at the next cottage to set down her washing. "Half the village has it that my son goes about at night slipping coins under doors. Shillings to every man who coughs at his loom, every widow with a tale to tell." He laughed, short and unpleasant. "A soft fool's charity. You'll empty your pockets and they'll despise you for it, and they'll be right to." Jonathan grinned into his wheat. Francis said nothing for a long moment, and when he raised his head, the look he gave his father was tired.

"I won't argue it with you, sir," he said. "I've given that up. But I'll ask you once, plainly, here where people can hear it. Set aside a fund. A small one. Enough that a man laid up with fever doesn't come back to find his children hungry. It would cost you less than the mare." Mr. Ashworth's answer came before the question had finished settling. "No. Not a penny, not now, not ever." Francis nodded slowly, as if he had expected nothing else. "Then I'll take it to the council," he said, "and they can refuse me there, in the minutes, with their names beside it." His father stared at him, wheeled the grey mare around, and rode off without another word, the dust of the lane rising behind him.

The gate creaked. Jane Bolton stood in it with her sleeves rolled to the elbow and flour on her wrists, and from her face she had heard every word. She did not open it wider. She looked first at Francis and his apples. "You mean well, Francis. I believe that. But a man who hides behind his father's coat is no man to lean on, and the money you creep about with at night is your father's money all the same." Then she turned to Jonathan, who had begun to lift the beribboned sheaf toward her. "And you can put that down. You've stood here grinning while another man was shamed, and you'll go to the Crown tonight and tell it louder than it happened. Noise is not courage, Jonathan."

Neither man found an answer. Jane laid one hand on the top rail of the gate. "The widow Marsh was put out of her cottage with her rent paid and her husband not a month in the ground, and every man in Hollowmere knows who did it and how. I'll not walk out with either of you until one of you makes that right. Not talks of it. Makes it right." She stepped back and shut the gate firmly. The apples and the wheat stayed in the lane where the two men had stood, and both men went off in opposite directions without looking at each other.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="cd62b00b-22a6-46fe-acab-cba1b7d4391c">Jane walked down to the widow Marsh's cottage to see whether either man would come.</li>
<li edge="50ecdbcc-47e3-4efa-b3bd-1ff42e84f9d7">Francis went up to the council chamber to put his plea for a fund before the council.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="e009bc3e-5d42-4e31-bb66-91f920b4884f" -->

The widow Marsh's cottage stood at the low end of Hollowmere, where the lane gave up its stones and became mud, and by mid-morning half the village had gathered along its hedge. The bailiff was a broad man in a brown coat who did his work without hurry. He carried out a rush-bottomed chair first, then a kettle, then a bolster with its ticking split, and set each thing down on the wet grass as if he were laying a table. The widow sat on her own doorstep with her hands folded in her apron and did not look at any of it. A thin rain had begun, and it beaded on the kettle's lid.

At the gate stood Squire Ashby in his riding cloak, and at his elbow his son Francis, bareheaded, very straight, his gloves held in one fist. The squire spoke now and then to the bailiff in a low, reasonable voice, naming what was to go. Francis said nothing at all. When the bailiff came out bearing a small oak press, the only good piece in the house, Francis's mouth opened a little, and closed again. He looked at the press and then at the hedge, and his eyes went along the faces there until they found Jane Pellow's, and stayed there, and asked something of her that she did not answer.

From the thick of the crowd came Jonathan Reeve's voice, loud enough to turn heads. He called the bailiff a thief and a dog's son, and swore that any man who laid hands on a widow's bed would answer for it some dark night on the Coldharbour road. Those near him shifted and murmured, and one or two laughed. But he kept his place behind the hedge, with a row of shoulders between him and the gate, and when the bailiff glanced his way and went on working, Jonathan only shouted the louder, and the words went up into the rain and came to nothing. The bed frame came out in two pieces. The widow's cat ran out after it and away under the hedge.

Jane watched until the last of it, a cracked jug with a sprig of dried lavender still in it, lay on the grass beside the rest. Then she drew her shawl over her hair and turned up the lane. Francis came after her as far as the bend, and said her name, and held out his arm; she walked past it as if it were a branch. At the stile Jonathan caught her up, flushed and breathing hard, and offered his own, saying the mud was bad there. She climbed the stile without it, lifted her skirts clear of the mire, and went on alone toward the village, and neither man followed her farther than the place where the other stood.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c9a14e98-0ce0-4545-9287-dfd85c38740d">The next morning, both Francis and Jonathan walked up the lane to the gate of Jane's farm.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="79e0430d-8056-4053-918a-87d12ef14a41" -->

The lane that ran past the Bolton farm was still wet with dew when the two young men came up it, one from either end, and stopped a few yards apart as if neither had expected the other. Jane Bolton was already at the gate. She had her sleeves pushed to the elbow and a pail set down by her feet, and she did not open the latch for either of them. Jonathan Pryce took off his cap and began to speak, but she cut across him before he had got three words out.

"Shouting from the middle of a crowd is not courage, Jonathan," she said. "Any man can be brave with forty others at his back and none of them looking at his face." Jonathan's ears went red, and he turned his cap round in his hands and said nothing. Then she looked at Francis Hale, who stood a little behind the gatepost with his hands clasped, and her voice did not soften. "And I'll not marry a man who stands silent behind his father. I won't. I'll walk out with neither of you. Not to church, not to the fair, not down this lane, until one of you shows me he's his own man. Put right what was done to the widow Marsh. Then come back."

Jonathan went first, with a short nod and his jaw set, his boots slapping the puddles all the way to the bend. Francis stayed. Jane came round the gate and stood close enough that he had to look at her. "You owe him nothing," she said, low. "Not your tongue, not your conscience. You know what's right as well as I do." Francis opened his mouth and closed it again. Twice while she spoke his eyes slid past her shoulder and down the lane toward the Hale fields, as though a tall figure in a grey coat might come walking up between the hedges at any moment. None did. The lane stayed empty but for a wagtail picking at the ruts. At last he said, almost too quietly to hear, "I know it. But I still want him to say I've done well. I've wanted that all my life, Jane. I can't make it stop."

She looked at him a long moment, picked up her pail, and went back through the gate without a word, and the latch fell shut behind her. That night, after the lamps in the Bolton kitchen had gone out, Francis saddled the bay mare by lantern light and rode down the same lane in the dark, past the gate where she had stood, toward his father's house. He meant to ask Benedict Hale for his blessing first. He would take up her challenge, and he would seek her hand, but not until his father had told him he might.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="0c4d1796-00e4-48c6-9e5d-f61e36b113b5">Francis went to the manor court in the Hollowmere church hall on the morning the widow Marsh's cottage came before the bailiff.</li>
<li edge="38af73ce-3867-4bb9-befb-a89c4f0a244e">Francis rode from the county assizes to his father's study at Hallowmere, to stand before Benedict and ask for his blessing.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="f2eef4f7-5f03-4d8d-8846-7a5ab7f68221" -->

The church hall at Hollowmere smelled of damp hassocks and tallow, and the manor court filled it to the walls. Farmers stood shoulder to shoulder with weavers from the mill, their caps crushed in their hands, while the bailiff sat at the trestle table beneath the window with the court roll open before him and a ring of iron keys at his elbow. Among the keys was the one to the widow Marsh's cottage. The widow sat near the stove with her bundle on her knees, and Jane Ashby sat beside her, holding her hand and looking at no one. Twice Francis Holloway's eyes went to her, and twice she turned her face toward the widow as though she had not felt them.

Francis rose before the bailiff had finished reading. He was younger than most of the men in the room, and his voice cracked once on the first word, but it steadied. "My father's order is set aside," he said. "The cottage is to be returned to Mrs. Marsh today, with her furniture and her rent forgiven to Lady Day. Enter it in the roll." A murmur ran along the benches. The bailiff looked up, pen lifted, as if he expected some older and more familiar voice to contradict this one, but none came. At last he dipped the pen and wrote. He slid the key across the table, and the widow began to cry without making any sound. Jane did not look up, though her hand tightened on the old woman's.

Francis did not sit. His coat pocket held a fold of paper, a list of names in his own hand, Tom Pettigrew's cough, the Durrant girl's fever, three looms idle at Brook Row. Each name stood for a few shillings he had paid out and could not pay again. He crossed to the front bench, where Edmund Crane sat in a good grey coat with his gloves laid across one knee, and spoke low enough that only the nearest rows could hear. "There must be a fund for the weavers when they fall sick, raised by rate and kept by the council. My father will vote against it. I need yours, and the two that follow yours." Crane smiled the slow, unhurried smile of a man who has been waiting at a gate he knew would open. "And the canal, Mr. Holloway? The cut through Fen Acre that your father has blocked these three years. Will you speak for it when it comes before the council?" Francis heard him out to the end. He did not tell Crane what the cut would do to the water meadows, or whose cottages stood along Fen Acre. "I'll consider it," he said, and Crane drew on one glove, finger by finger, as though the matter were already settled.

From the back of the hall, Jonathan Pell's voice carried over the shuffle of boots. He leaned against the doorpost with his arms folded and an audience of two stable lads. "I'd have had that key off the bailiff before he'd sat down," he was saying, "and the old man told to his face what he was. That's how it's done. None of this bowing and scraping on the front bench." The stable lads nodded. Near the stove, Jane helped the widow to her feet and walked her toward the door with the iron key in her fist. She passed between both men and looked at neither of them.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="de6c0c10-bfce-4a0a-a341-98b321c1958e">Francis walked home to the manor hall to face his father, while Jane went off toward the widow's lane with the key.</li>
<li edge="3443d3f0-aede-42cf-8909-dba8c7ebb7ea">Francis left the church hall for Mill Lane, to sit up through the night with one of the sick weavers on his list.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0e3be3a4-19bc-46d7-9eb1-f5e8bcf07bf9" -->

The hall at Tomson manor was cold even in September, its great hearth unlit and its long oak table bare but for the squire's hat, which he had flung down on entering. Squire Tomson did not sit. He stood beneath the antlers his grandfather had nailed above the mantel, red to the roots of his grey hair, and when his son came in behind him he rounded on the boy as if he had been waiting all the road home to do it.

"Before the whole village," the squire said. "Before Coombe and the miller and every gossiping wife in Hollowmere, you stood up and told them my word was wrong. My bailiff's seizure, set aside by my own son. Do you know what they will say at the Bell tonight?" He struck the table with the flat of his hand. "You will go back down there tomorrow and tell them you spoke in haste. You will say the widow Marsh's goods stay where they are until her rent is paid. Or you will not have a stick of this house when I am gone. Not a field, not a chair. I will leave it to the Church first."

Francis Tomson stood very still by the door. He was a tall young man, plainly dressed, and the mud of the green was still on his boots. "Her rent was paid, sir," he said. "Partly. And the rest was owed by a man who has been dead since Lady Day. You cannot take a widow's bed for her husband's debt and call it law." His father opened his mouth, and Francis inclined his head, not unkindly. "Disinherit me, then. But her table and her bed are lying in Hadley's barn with the rats at them, and she has slept two nights on straw. I'll fetch them back myself." He took his coat from the peg, went out, and did not close the door behind him, and the squire was left alone with the antlers and the cold hearth and the sound of his son's boots going down the gravel toward the stables.

Beyond the manor's yard wall, at the well where the lane bent toward the village, Jane Ashby had set down her bucket to draw. She had heard the shouting through the open hall window, as everyone within a furlong must have, and she was still listening for more when Jonathan Pryce came strolling up from the direction of the smithy with his thumbs in his waistcoat. He leaned on the well-curb beside her as if he owned it. "Fine noise up there," he said, with a nod toward the hall. "Francis making a show of himself. I'd have had the old woman's things back in her cottage before the bailiff's cart was out of the lane, if it had been me. Never would have let it come to a meeting. Talk's cheap; it's doing that counts." He smiled at her, waiting to be admired.

Jane looked at him a long moment, then at the hall window, then down the lane where a cart was already rattling out of the Tomson stableyard with Francis on the bench and the reins in his own hands. She drew up her bucket, set it on the curb, and left it there. "Then I expect I'll find you both at the widow's," she said, and she gathered her skirts and walked off down the widow's lane without looking back, to see for herself which of the two men she would find there with his sleeves rolled up.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="69199950-bb5b-4865-b4bf-b94422d5ed2f">Francis drove the rattling cart down the lane toward Hadley's barn, where the widow's table and bed lay among the rats.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="56b76373-34f1-49bc-a915-bd3dd7475ee5" -->

The bailiff's barn smelled of damp straw and tallow, and the widow Marsh's furniture stood in the gloom where it had been dragged: a deal table with one leg shimmed by a folded hymn sheet, two rush chairs, a press with its doors tied shut by twine, and a cradle no one had slept in for twenty years. Haskett, the bailiff, planted himself before it all with his thumbs in his waistcoat and said, for the third time, that nothing left that barn without the squire's word. Francis Tomson did not argue a fourth time. He drew out his purse, counted the sum onto the lid of a grain bin, coin by coin, until the full amount of the disputed debt lay there in the dusty light, and then he took off his coat, hung it on a nail, and lifted the first chair into the cart himself.

Jonathan had come in by the field path, the same narrow way Jane had taken, and he stood near the door watching Francis work without offering a hand. When the sound of a horse came up from the road, hooves hard on the frozen ruts and slowing at the gate, he did not wait to see who rode it. He was gone between one breath and the next, out through the gap in the back boards, and only the swing of a loose plank showed where he had been.

The rider was Benedict Tomson, mud to the knees of his breeches from the London road, and he came into the barn without dismounting at first, ducking his head under the lintel and looking down at his son, who had the table half onto the tailboard. A letter showed at the edge of his coat, the paper soft and creased from handling. He climbed down at last and said that he had read it. Twice. He said he had stopped a long while over the line about the empty seat in the chapel, and for a moment his voice seemed to be looking for some other road to take. Then it found the old one. The weavers' case, he said, was a betrayal of every man who held land in this county, and the Wood girl's people were what they had always been, and no letter would make them fit to stand under the Tomson name. Whatever warmer thing had been riding with him from London, he did not say it. He said three things only, each shorter than the last: that he would not come to Ashbury, that he would not send his blessing there, and that Francis need not look for him at the church door.

Francis heard him out with the table still in his hands. He set it in the cart, went back for the press, and then for the cradle, and lashed the load down with the bailiff's rope while Haskett pocketed the coins and found something to study in the rafters. Not a word passed between father and son. When the last knot was pulled tight, Francis took his coat from the nail, climbed to the seat, and shook out the reins, and the cart rolled out of the yard and turned up toward the widow's lane, the cradle rocking a little with every rut. Benedict stood a moment in the empty barn. Then he swung back into the saddle, set his heels hard to his horse, and rode after it.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="5feb176c-c75a-45b4-9867-7c06a0d0ec8f">The cart rolled on up the widow's lane, with Benedict riding hard behind it toward the Marsh cottage.</li>
<li edge="71a466cd-a6d0-47d2-9dd6-224815985a78">The days ran on toward Ashbury, where Francis spent the eve of his wedding under the Ashbys' roof.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="ea8c9218-5932-4cda-847f-f76e63efcc04" -->

The widow Marsh's furniture stood in the lane where the bailiff's men had left it: a deal table with one short leg, two rush-bottomed chairs, a dresser whose plates had been stacked on the seat of a settle, and a rag rug rolled and tied with twine. A thin rain had darkened all of it. Francis took off his coat, laid it over the low wall, and lifted the dresser by its sides. He carried it through the narrow doorway sideways, scraping his knuckles on the frame, and came back for the chairs. Jane stood by the gate with her shawl drawn up over her bonnet and watched him go in and out, in and out, and did not offer to help, because she understood that he needed to do it himself.

His father arrived on horseback before the table was moved. He did not dismount. "You will put that back where you found it," he said, "and you will go to Mr. Ashby tomorrow and tell him the ruling was a mistake, and that you mean to have it set aside." Francis set down the chairs he was holding. "The ruling was not a mistake, sir. The lease was good. The court said so, and I won't unsay it." The old man's face went a mottled red, and the horse sidled under him. "You'll make me a laughingstock to every tenant from here to Wexley." "Then they'll know you can be laughed at and still be just," said Francis, and he picked up the chairs again.

Mr. Crane had ridden in behind the elder man and sat his own horse with the ease of someone watching a play he had paid good money for. When Francis came out the second time, Crane leaned down. "Since you're in a mood for setting things right, Marsh, set this right as well. The canal company wants your name on the subscription, and your father's money behind it. Half the county is waiting to see which way you go." Francis wiped his hands on his breeches and looked down the lane toward the mill, where the weavers' cottages stood in a crooked row along the water. "I'll put my name to it," he said, "if the cut goes north of the mill and leaves Mill Lane standing. Every cottage. Draw the route that way, and you'll have me." Crane laughed outright. "Scruples, at a canal meeting. You'll lose us a quarter mile of good ground for a dozen families of weavers." But he pulled off his glove and held out his hand, and Francis took it and shook it once, firmly. "You may laugh," Francis said. "A fund that's actually raised will feed people this winter. A fine refusal won't feed anybody."

Jonathan had come up the lane on foot while all this went on, and he stood at Jane's elbow talking without a pause. He would have gone to the magistrate first. He would never have let the bailiffs touch a stick of it. He would have told Crane to his face that the canal could go to the devil, and his father would have thanked him for it in the end. Jane nodded now and then and did not take her eyes off Francis, who had got the table up onto his shoulder and was carrying it in, short leg and all, while his father sat silent on his horse and the rain went on falling.

When the lane was empty but for the wet marks where the furniture had stood, Francis came to the gate for his coat. He hesitated with it in his hands, as though he expected to be sent away. Jane put back her shawl. "If you are walking toward the church," she said, "I am going that way myself." Jonathan was still talking as the two of them went off together down the lane, and neither of them turned round to hear the rest.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="684065b1-e871-4314-a51c-91bac6d51744">Jane walked on toward the church at Francis's side, with Jonathan still hurrying after them down the lane.</li>
<li edge="0ae89e6a-2887-4cf0-bcbd-f686edaaca29">Francis stood at the chapel door on his wedding morning, watching the lane for his father.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1e568d6a-779d-4c7a-8a33-dfbdf2d81069" -->

The bells had fallen silent over Hollowmere, and the congregation spilled out among the leaning headstones, the women shaking out their shawls and the men settling their hats against a wind that smelled of wet hay. Jane walked beside Francis along the gravel path that wound past the yew, and they had nearly reached the lychgate when Jonathan came striding after them, his good coat buttoned to the throat and his cheeks bright from the cold.

"You should have heard him in there," Jonathan said, planting himself in their way. "Old Tomson, praying loud as a bull, as though the Almighty owed him rent. If he had spoken so to me, I'd have stood up in the pew and told him what he is. I'd have looked him in the eye and not blinked once. A man like that only wants someone to stand up to him, and he'll fold like wet paper." He struck his palm with his fist, pleased with the sound of it, and looked from one face to the other for agreement.

Jane did not give it. She drew her gloves tighter over her fingers and regarded him with a steadiness that made him shift his feet on the gravel. "You've told us that before, Jonathan," she said. "You've told half the parish. I only wonder why it is always what you would have done, and never what you did. Tomson walked past you not a quarter hour since, and you were studying the weathercock."

Jonathan opened his mouth, and closed it, and opened it again, but whatever he meant to say would not come. Francis had stood quiet through all of it, his hands in his pockets and his eyes on the low grey roofs beyond the churchyard wall. He said nothing in his own defence, nor in Jonathan's, nor in praise of himself. Instead he turned to Jane. "Will you walk with me to the widow Pell's?" he asked. "I've been at her roof all week. My father let the thatch rot through these three winters past, and the rain was coming in over her bed. It's near done now. I'd like you to see it." Jane looked at him a long moment, then took his arm, and the two of them passed out through the lychgate and down the lane, leaving Jonathan alone among the stones with the wind tugging at his fine buttoned coat.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="56c0d69b-541c-46b5-80c5-681f1479eed5">Francis and Jane went on down the lane to the widow's cottage at the low end of Hollowmere, and Jonathan, after a while, followed them.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="842cf774-eabf-4e1f-b315-00d2ae54386b" -->

The widow Marsh's cottage sat at the low end of Hollowmere, where the lane gave out into mud and the thatch hung so far over the door that a tall man had to stoop beneath it. That morning the whole village seemed to have found some reason to be near it. Women stood with baskets they had not filled, and men leaned on the churchyard wall with their caps in their hands. Old Tobias, the eldest of the weavers, stood nearest the gate. His knuckles were swollen like knots in a rope, and he did not lean on anything at all.

Benedict Tomson came down the lane on foot, his bailiff a pace behind him with a paper folded long and sealed in red. The old man's coat was good black wool, brushed clean, and he looked at the cottage as though he were already counting what the land would fetch without it. But Francis was standing in the doorway. Behind him, in the dim of the room, Jane Marsh held her shawl closed at the throat and did not come forward.

The bailiff held out the paper. Francis took it, broke the seal, and read it through once, slowly, so that everyone at the gate could watch him read. Then he tore it across, and across again, and the sound of it was very loud in the stillness of the lane. "This cottage is hers," he said, "for as long as she lives. No man will put her out of it." He did not raise his voice. He did not need to.

Then he looked at his father. For a long moment he waited, and the villagers waited with him, for a word or a nod or even a curse, anything that would show what Benedict Tomson thought of it. The old man's face gave nothing. His mouth stayed shut. The bailiff glanced from one to the other and found somewhere else to look.

Francis turned away from him. He stepped down from the doorway, crossed to the gate, and put the torn pieces of the notice into Tobias's crooked hands, closing the old weaver's fingers over them as if he were giving him something worth keeping. A murmur went along the churchyard wall. At the back of the crowd, half hidden by the hawthorn, Jonathan stood with his arms folded, having come only to watch. He watched still, and said nothing, and did not come any closer.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="764808ca-87b1-469d-92de-7528929fd440">Francis carried the widow's cause to the council chamber at Harrowgate, and the village followed him there.</li>
<li edge="ec5dcef5-dd1d-4c8d-ba81-e0faaee00d30">Francis went on to the chapel at Ashbury, and the weavers followed him there.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="074060dc-f454-4f65-8bf4-154d70e73869" -->

The council chamber at Harrowgate smelled of damp wool and tallow, and the long oak table had been polished so many years by so many sleeves that it shone like dark water under the gaslights. Twelve aldermen sat along it, and behind the rail the gallery was packed with mill hands in their Sunday coats, caps twisted in their fists. The widow Marsh sat at the end of the front bench with her hands folded over a black reticule, looking at nothing. When Francis rose to speak, his father, old Mr. Ashworth, did not turn his head, but the knuckles of his right hand went white on the head of his cane.

Francis said it plainly. He would vote for Mr. Crane's canal, every lock and cutting of it, and he would not oppose the compulsory purchases along the Hollowmere bank, provided that Mr. Crane and his friends on the council voted in turn for a sick-pay fund drawn from the mill rates, and for the return of the Hollowmere cottage to Mrs. Marsh, whose husband had died under a fallen beam at Crane's own dye works. Edmund Crane, thin and sleek in a grey waistcoat, smiled as if he had expected nothing else and inclined his head. It was then that Mr. Ashworth stood. "A coward's bargain," he said, loud enough for the gallery to hear. "You sell the town to that man for a handful of shillings and a hovel. I did not raise you to crawl to Crane." Francis looked at him for a long moment. "Crane will grow rich on his canal, Father. He would have grown rich without me. This way forty families eat this winter, and Mrs. Marsh sleeps under her own roof. If that is cowardice, I will carry the name." The vote was called. Hands rose along the oak table, slowly, then all at once, and the motion carried eight to four. Somewhere in the gallery a woman began to cry, and the mill hands broke into a ragged cheer that the clerk did nothing to stop. Mr. Ashworth took up his hat and went out without a word, his cane striking the floorboards like a clock.

When the chamber had half emptied and the lamplighter was moving along the corridor outside, Jane Hollis came down from the gallery and crossed to where Francis stood gathering his papers. She had watched him for two years defer to his father in every matter that counted, and she had told him so once, coldly, on the steps of the Assembly Rooms. Now she stood before him with her gloves in one hand and said nothing at all, and it was Francis who spoke, there beside the table with the clerk still blotting the minutes, asking her the question he had been afraid to ask. "Yes," she said. "Today you stood up for what was right, and you did it as your own man. I'd marry that man." She put her bare hand in his, and the clerk, without looking up, found something very urgent to do with his inkwell.

Jonathan Pryce had been leaning in the doorway the whole time, as he had leaned in every doorway that season, telling anyone near him how he would have handled Crane: how he would have exposed the man, broken the scheme, shamed the whole council into decency with one speech. He came forward now, hat in hand, and for once the speech did not come. "Well," he said, and cleared his throat. "I always had plenty to say about what I'd have done." He offered his hand to Francis, then bowed to Jane. "I wish you both happy. Truly. She chose the better man, and I'd be a fool to pretend otherwise." Then he put on his hat and walked out past the long table and the cooling lamps, and his footsteps went down the stone stairs into the Harrowgate night.

<!-- cell: markdown id="f1d4a92f-6fae-4e45-9ca6-f17d6c0a7ea1" -->

The weaving floor of Tomson mill shook with the noise of forty looms. The shuttles went back and forth so fast the eye lost them, and the air was white with lint that settled on bonnets and eyelashes and on the iron rail of the gallery where Benedict Tomson stood with his hands folded over the head of his cane. He had laid the first stone of the place himself, thirty years before, and on the bench of the Harrowgate court he still sat most Thursdays. Below him the foreman, a heavy man named Crabtree, had stopped at the seventh loom. The woman there was shaking so hard she could barely tie off a broken thread. Her cheeks burned the dull red of fever, and her lips had cracked at the corners.

"Three hours late and the warp half spoilt," Crabtree said, loud enough for the gallery to hear. "That's a week docked, Mrs. Pell. You'll thank me it's not your place." He wrote it in his ledger with a stub of pencil and licked the point afterward. Up on the rail Benedict gave a single slow nod, the same nod he gave from the bench when a sentence pleased him.

Francis Tomson had come down the iron stair with a sheaf of accounts under his arm, and he stopped at the foot of it. For a moment the words rose in him: that a sick woman was not a spoiled warp, that the ledger was a coward's instrument. He held them back. He knew how it would go. His father would back the foreman, the foreman would make an example of the woman, and the shouting would cost her more than the week. So he crossed the floor between the looms without hurrying. He took the small envelope of his own wages from his waistcoat, counted out what Crabtree had written down, and pressed the coins into Martha Pell's damp hand. He folded her fingers over them before she could protest. She stared at him. The loom beside her went on clattering, and no one else on the floor seemed to breathe.

Then he climbed the stair to the gallery. His father did not look at him, only at the floor below, as if it were a map of everything he owned. "I've read the eviction lists for Cotter's Row," Francis said. His voice was low, and it was steady. "Fourteen families, set for your bench next month. I won't be taking my seat in the counting house, Father. I'm going to stand for them in court." The cane's ferrule ground slowly against the boards. When Benedict turned at last, his face was very still. "Then you have chosen to betray this house," he said, "and you will do it without my blessing." Below them the shuttles kept on, and the lint drifted down between father and son.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="bd671341-b7f1-4866-92e3-e6811098dc2f">Francis carried a cashbox to the back room of the Red Lion, where weavers with docked wages had begun to gather.</li>
<li edge="7a089585-320f-401d-9c42-c2cc38a7aa14">Francis went down Weavers' Row at dusk to the evicted widow Clara Ashby's doorstep, looking for proof against his father.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="507cd76c-40e1-4702-a700-d026f3317a0b" -->

The back room of the Red Lion smelled of tallow and wet wool, and the fire in the grate gave off more smoke than heat. Francis sat at the long table with a ledger open before him and a cashbox at his elbow, and the weavers came to him one at a time out of the passage where they had been waiting. A woman with a rag knotted round her throat set down a scrap of paper on which the overseer had written *three days, fever*, and Francis counted out the shillings the mill had kept back. A boy of perhaps fourteen, coughing into his sleeve, showed him a docket for two days. Behind them the line stretched out into the taproom: pale faces, blotched hands, men who leaned against the doorframe because standing straight cost them too much.

Before the next man could step up, Tom Rigby pushed through from the back. He was a broad, red-whiskered fellow with a voice made for shouting over looms, and he planted both fists on the table among the coins. "This is charity, Mr. Francis, and charity's a bucket under a leaking roof," he said. "Hollis docks a sick man and you pay him, and Hollis laughs all the way to the bank. Say the word and every shed in Harrowgate goes quiet tomorrow. They'll listen if you stand with us. Back a walkout and we'll have that mill on its knees inside a week."

The room had gone still. Francis closed the ledger, keeping one finger in the page, and looked up at him. "Hollis has a hundred hands waiting at the gate for every one of you, Tom," he said. "You walk out tomorrow and the day after you're all sacked, and there's men from Bradford in your places by Monday, and I can't pay wages to a whole town that has none. No. I'll not back it." Rigby opened his mouth, but Francis went on, quieter. "Here's what I will do. I'll go on covering whatever's docked for sickness, every penny, for as long as it takes. And I'll go to Hollis and the other masters and put it to them plain that there's to be a proper sick fund, paid into regular, so a man with a fever isn't robbed for having one. That's slower than a walkout. But you'll all still have your looms at the end of it."

For a moment Rigby stood scowling down at the coins. Then the woman with the rag at her throat said, "God bless you, sir," and someone at the door took it up, and then the whole line was talking at once. Rigby straightened, rubbed his whiskers, and finally gave a short laugh and clapped Francis on the shoulder hard enough to rattle the cashbox. By the time the last docket had been paid, the weavers had spilled out through the taproom and onto the street, and they were telling it to anyone who would stop. Francis would pay them, Francis would make the masters build a fund, Francis had said Hollis could whistle for his docked shillings. Their voices carried up the hill past the chapel and the mill gates, under the windows of the counting house, and on into the dark of the town.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="0c9e7116-804c-4a91-b9ae-2f8b6ec0498a">The talk spread past the town to Hollowmere village, and Francis set off down the lane to the Bolton cottage with a basket of apples on his arm.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="a067e0e9-cb65-4e39-9675-559d7fb643ca" -->

The council chamber at Harrowgate smelled of wet wool and lamp oil, for the rain had come in on every coat, and the gallery above was packed with men who had walked up from the weavers' rows with handkerchiefs pressed to their mouths. The fever had been in those streets three weeks. Everyone in the room knew it, and nobody on the benches below had yet said the word aloud. Francis Thorne stood at the clerk's table with his motion in his hand and read it out in a voice that did not quite hold steady: a fund, raised from the rates, to pay a sick weaver some part of his wage while he lay abed, so that a man need not choose between the loom and the grave.

He had cut it down twice before the chamber sat, and he cut it again on his feet when the murmuring started. No inspection of the mills, no limit on the hours, no mention of the drains. Only the fund, bare as a deal board. Mr. Hale rose before he had finished, gaunt and buttoned to the throat, and told the council that he would not dirty his hands with charity dressed up as reform. "A sick-pay fund," he said, "is a bandage for a wound the mills will open again tomorrow. Bring me a ten-hour bill, Mr. Thorne, or bring me nothing." The gallery cheered him, and Francis felt the heat climb into his face, because he knew Hale was right and knew too that a ten-hour bill would not carry a single vote in that room.

Then Benedict Thorne got up from the owners' bench, and the chamber went quiet in the way it did for money. He did not look at his son. He spoke of trade, of the price of yarn from Lancashire, of the burden on honest ratepayers, and he called the weavers' complaints idle grievances, got up by agitators among men who would sooner drink than work. When he sat down there was a dry rattle of approval from the bench around him. Francis took a bundle of papers from his coat. They were the eviction records Clara Ashby had copied out in her small, careful hand from the agent's books, and he read them name by name: the Pollards, put out with a child in fever; the widow Marsh, put out the week her husband was buried; eleven households in Tanner's Row since Lady Day, every one of them on a mill rent. He read until his father's jaw was set like a vice, and then he read three more.

It was not enough. When the count began, Francis saw that it would fall short by four, and he saw Alderman Crane lean back in his chair and lift one finger, idly, as a man might at an auction. Everyone knew what Crane's votes cost: a word in the right ear on the new gasworks contract, a favour remembered later. Francis met his eye and nodded. Crane's four hands went up together, and the clerk called the motion carried. In the gallery someone began to clap and then thought better of it. Hale gathered his papers and walked past Francis without a glance.

Benedict Thorne rose before the chair had closed the sitting. He took his hat from the peg by the door, settled it on his head, and went out into the rain without a word or a look for his son, and the door swung shut behind him on its weighted hinge. Francis stood among the scraping chairs with Clara's papers still in his hand, and the sting of it sat in his chest like a swallowed stone. He folded the records along their creases, slowly, and put them away. He would go home tonight, he decided, and he would stand in his father's study and ask for his blessing to marry her. Whether it was given or not, he meant to have asked.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c578f2cb-4e64-4fa1-8ac2-ad82031aa1d4">Francis walked down into the weavers' rows, to the Dobbs cottage where a sick weaver lay abed.</li>
<li edge="2adabf52-4a84-47b8-a8ad-e00abbf832cb">Francis rode out to Hollowmere, to the lane by the Bolton farm gate where Jane Bolton stood.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="cdf48d48-f99c-433f-914e-bf44e27171a8" -->

The Dobbs cottage stood fourth in the row, its single window clouded with the breath of a fire kept too low to warm anyone. Inside, the loom took up half the room, silent, a length of grey worsted still stretched across it where Tom Dobbs had left off three days before. He lay now on a straw tick by the hearth, his face the colour of tallow, shivering under two coats and a shawl. His wife sat beside him on an upturned crate with the rent book open on her knee, staring at it as though the figures might change if she looked long enough. In the corner a child coughed, stopped, and coughed again.

Francis Ashby stood just inside the door, hat in his hands, and counted coins from his purse onto the edge of the loom: the quarter's rent, and then the doctor's fee, and then a little more for coal. Mrs. Dobbs did not reach for the money. She looked at it, then at him, and said that she could not pay it back, not this year nor the next. Francis told her he had not asked her to, and set the rent book on top of the coins so they would not slide off.

Hale had followed him down the row and now leaned in the doorway, filling it, his coat collar turned up against the damp. He watched the coins with open contempt. "You know what you're buying," he said. "You keep Dobbs alive so he can go back to Garside's frames for nine shillings a week. Every penny of charity is a penny the masters don't have to pay. You're propping up the mills with your own wages, and calling it kindness." Behind him, in the lane, old Mr. Ashby had come up with his cane and his heavy black umbrella, and he added his own verdict without lowering his voice: it was soft-headed folly, he said, and a man who gave away his wages to every cough in Harrowgate would end with nothing to give and no one grateful for it. Francis heard them both out. Then he shrugged, put his hat back on, and told Mrs. Dobbs that the doctor would come before nightfall.

The doctor came, and so did the talk. It ran along the weavers' rows faster than the fever had, from doorstep to doorstep, carried by women at the pump and men coming off the evening shift: young Ashby had paid for Dobbs. Before the lamps were lit there was a knock at the Dobbs cottage asking whether Mr. Ashby was still within, and then another. A girl with a baby on her hip. A man whose brother could not rise from his bed. A widow from the far end of the row who said nothing at all, only stood in the rain and waited. Francis went out to them one by one, and the line in the lane grew longer while Hale and his father looked on.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="9788f0f5-d9a6-40ca-8f81-d6cc7cf2e0c7">Francis left the line in the lane and went to the manor court sitting in the Hollowmere church hall.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="b517de35-ab74-47f1-baca-23be65bfa861" -->

The cottage on Mill Lane had one room below and one above, and the weaver lay in the lower, on a pallet dragged close to the hearth so that the silent loom stood over him like a scaffold. His name was Amos Pell, and the fever had him talking to men who were not there. Francis sat on a three-legged stool and wrung out cloths in a basin of well water, laying them across the old man's forehead as each one warmed. Near midnight he went out to the garden for more water, and by the light of the lantern he saw the stakes: pale new timber, each with a strip of red rag tied at the top, driven in a straight line through the cabbages and the bean rows and on through the next garden and the one beyond it, as far as the light would reach. Someone had walked down Mill Lane with a chain and a theodolite and had not troubled to knock.

The preacher came an hour later, Mr. Hollis of the chapel, his coat dark with drizzle and a Bible under his arm for the sick man's sake. He looked at Amos, then at the stakes through the window, then at Francis. "You see now what Crane is," he said, low, so as not to wake the patient. "He will cut this lane in half and call it progress. Stand up on Sunday and say so. Say there will be no bargain with him, none, and the whole town will stand with you." Francis heard him out. He looked at the loom, which had not moved in a fortnight, and at the empty meal crock on the shelf, and at the old man's hands lying open on the blanket. A refusal would be a fine thing to say from the chapel steps. It would not pull one stake out of the ground, and it would not put bread in that crock. "If the canal is coming," he said at last, "then I would rather argue where it goes than argue whether it goes. Let it bend round the gardens, and let the men who dig it be hired from this lane." Mr. Hollis said nothing for a long moment. Then he prayed over Amos, briefly and not unkindly, and went back out into the rain.

When the house was quiet again Francis fetched paper and ink from his coat and set them on the lid of the meal crock, which he balanced on his knees. He had written to his father before, and he knew the answer. Still he could not leave it there. He began, *My dear Father,* and then crossed it out, and began again with only *Father.* He wrote of the weavers and of their case: how it had been put plainly and fairly, without a stone thrown or a window broken, and how even now, when it must come to terms, it would come to them honestly and not for any man's private gain. He wrote of Clara's people, of the bed they had made up for him without being asked, of her mother's table, of her brother lending him a coat when his own was soaked through. Between paragraphs he rose to change the cloth on Amos's brow, and twice he held the old man's head while he drank.

Toward morning the fever seemed to loosen its grip, and Amos slept without muttering. Francis wrote the last page more slowly than the rest. *I am to be married at the chapel on Mill Lane,* he wrote. *I would have you there above anyone. There is a seat kept for you in the front pew, and it will stay empty for no one else. Come, Father, and give us your blessing, or send it if you cannot come, but do not send nothing.* He signed it, folded it, and sealed it with a drop of candle wax pressed flat under his thumb. When the grey light showed the stakes standing in the wet gardens like a line of mourners, he walked the letter down to the inn yard and put it into the carrier's hand himself, and watched the cart go off up the road toward the town where his father lived.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="7665e428-0568-4fdb-9244-bd0e1e2ded4b">Francis carried a folded survey of the canal's line to his father's study in Harrowgate.</li>
<li edge="f0e89a44-6f54-426a-9e43-cdbb15ea94f2">Francis went to the bailiff's barn at Hollowmere, where the widow Marsh's furniture was held.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5a8d6066-6760-4b62-8b00-b1e62333ce65" -->

The study smelled of tallow and old leather, and the fire had burned down to a red seam beneath the grate. Francis Tomson cleared his father's desk of ledgers without asking, stacking them on the floor beside the fender, and unrolled the survey across the green baize. It was a fair copy on heavy paper, still creased from where it had been folded small enough to pass from one coat pocket to another. In the lower corner, in a cramped clerk's hand, were the words *Crane & Partners, Harrowgate Cut, Line of Intended Works.* He weighted the curling edges with an inkstand, a brass candlestick, and two of his father's books on drainage, and bent close.

The canal ran as a double line in red ink, straight and indifferent, down from the turnpike and across Mill Lane, where a row of small black squares stood in its path. Francis set his fingertip on the lane and drew it slowly westward, away from the red, down the gentle fall of the land to where the map was lettered *Tomson: Water Meadow*. He followed the curve of the brook through the meadow and brought his finger back to meet the red line beyond the last of the cottages. The detour was perhaps half a mile longer. It would want another lock, and a deeper cutting at the meadow's head, and a good deal more of Crane's money.

His father had watched all this from the wing chair by the hearth without rising. Now he leaned forward, and the firelight caught the gray stubble along his jaw. "You'd give him the meadow," he said. "My father drained that ground with his own spade. And you'd hand it to a man you call a thief in a public house, so he can dig his stinking ditch through it." He spat the words softly, which was worse than shouting. "That's a weakling's folly, Francis. Your hands were clean of that canal. Keep them so."

Francis did not look up from the map. He traced the new line once more, pressing hard enough that his nail left a faint furrow in the paper. "Clean hands," he said, "have never kept a roof over anyone. Not over us, and not over the families on Mill Lane." He straightened at last, and the candle threw his shadow long across the ceiling. "Crane wants his cut finished before the frost. He can have it finished, and he can have it through our meadow, and he'll pay what it costs to go round. That's my price."

His father said nothing more. Francis took up a pencil and began, carefully, to draw the route in for himself.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="72671dbb-3802-4640-a46b-3d32635a131b">Francis rolled up the survey and walked down to the lane outside the widow Marsh's cottage in Hollowmere.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="8b4883e6-dd41-4d2e-a3d9-7b9e3d721acd" -->

The chapel at Ashbury had been swept before dawn, and the smell of beeswax and cold stone hung under the low beams. Someone had threaded late roses through the ends of the pews, and the front one on the groom's side stood empty, its cushion plumped and untouched, kept for Benedict. Francis stood at the door in his good coat with his gloves crushed in one hand, watching the lane. The weavers' boy found him there, breathless and mud to the knee. Crane's surveyors had been out since first light, he said. They had driven their stakes in a line straight through the row of cottages below the mill, red rags tied to each one, the last of them hammered into old Mrs. Pell's garden among her beans.

Francis said nothing for a moment. Then he crossed to the porch, where Crane was shaking the rain from his hat and talking to the vicar about the weather. Francis asked for a word, and Crane followed him into the narrow space between the inner and outer doors, where the parish notices curled on their pins. Francis kept his voice low. The canal would have his vote and the fund would have its subscription, he said, but only if the line went round the cottages. If a single stake stayed where it was, he would stand up at the meeting and vote against the whole scheme, and he would take the others with him. Crane's jaw worked. He began to speak of gradients and costs. Francis cut him short and offered the common meadow on the far side of the brook. It was flat, it was cheap to cut, and the village had grazed it for two hundred years. Crane could have it. He would not have the houses. Crane studied him for a long moment, then put out his hand, and Francis took it before he could think better of the meadow.

Inside, the organ had begun, thin and a little out of tune. Francis walked to the rail and took his place, and as the vicar opened the book he turned his head once toward the door. The lane beyond it lay grey and wet and empty, and the front pew stayed bare.

Then the door swung wider, and the weavers came in. They came in their working clothes, some of them still smelling of wool and lanolin, women with shawls over their heads and men holding their caps. Mrs. Pell came on her son's arm. They did not crowd forward but filled the benches at the back behind Clara, row after row, until the little chapel was fuller than it had been at Easter, and they stood when the vicar raised his hand. Clara glanced back at them, and then at Francis, and something in her face eased. Francis looked once more past their heads to the open doorway. Then he turned his back on it, took Clara's hand, and spoke his vows clearly to her alone, without waiting for anyone else.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="bdfdb6af-1a1c-41be-a38a-5acc8ef362b0">Francis rode home to Harrowgate and went up to his father's study, with the bargain over the meadow still to answer for.</li>
<li edge="b5b153f0-1f35-43d6-ab6d-2dbde37d14b0">Francis went down to the widow Marsh's cottage at Hollowmere and stood in its doorway, with the villagers gathering at the gate.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="9ac48731-ffce-48de-836c-20e78feef001" -->

The study at the Tomson house in Harrowgate smelled of tallow and old ledgers, and the fire had been let burn low because the elder Tomson disliked waste even in his anger. A surveyor's plan lay unrolled across the desk, its corners held down by an inkstand, a brass rule, and two volumes of county statutes. A line of red ink crossed it from the river to the turnpike. The old line had run straight through the row of cottages at Dunning's End. The new one bent around them in a wide, careful curve, and to make room for the curve it swallowed the whole of the common meadow.

Josiah Tomson stood over the plan with one finger pressed on the meadow as though he could hold it in place. "Forty families graze that ground," he said, without looking up. "Their fathers grazed it. You sat at Crane's table and let him draw his ditch through it, and you came home and called it a victory." He raised his eyes then, and his voice dropped, which was worse than shouting. "There is a word for a man who saves a few roofs and gives away the bread. I will not dress it up for you. It is coward."

Francis did not answer at once. He was standing by the window, still in his riding coat, the mud of the Dunning road drying on his boots. "The roofs were not a few," he said. "There were eleven, with people inside them who had nowhere else to go. Crane would have had the line either way, Father. Parliament gave him the act. If I break with him tonight, he cuts through the cottages and the meadow both, and I lose any say I have in it." His father began to speak, and Francis lifted a hand, a thing he had never done in that room before. "You want me to stand on the commons with my arms folded and be ridden over, so that it will be said the Tomsons did not bend. That would be a fine thing to say. It would feed nobody."

He took a folded sheet from inside his coat and laid it on the plan, over the red line, over his father's finger. The paper was heavy and good, and Crane's seal was pressed into the wax at the foot of it. "Twelve pounds a year to every household with common right, for as long as the canal company stands, paid at Michaelmas through the vestry so that Crane cannot quietly forget it. His hand, his seal, and two witnesses." Francis waited while the old man read, and watched the anger in his face harden and then falter, because the sum was real and the signature was real. "It is a loss," Francis said quietly. "I know it is a loss. But a loss that is paid for is better than a noble defeat that leaves forty families poorer. You may call me what you like. I will not break with him." Josiah Tomson read the paper a second time. Then he let it lie where it was and turned to the fire, and he did not say the word again.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e30aaf31-86c8-47cc-a2d0-69447f142ab8">Francis went to the council chamber at the Harrowgate town hall, where the aldermen were to vote on Crane's canal.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="916c4c34-938e-426f-88a4-8973dee29afc" -->

The magistrates' court at Hallowmere sat above the corn exchange, and the smell of grain rose through the floorboards into a room that had not been aired since Michaelmas. Weavers filled the back benches in their Sunday coats, caps turning in their hands. At the front, beneath the faded royal arms, Benedict Hale sat with his spectacles low on his nose and the petition flat on the bench before him. He had read it through twice in silence while the clock above the door ticked. Below him, at the advocates' table, his son Francis stood with one hand resting on a stack of ledgers bound in string.

"The petition is dismissed," Benedict said. He did not raise his eyes from the paper. "The landlord acted within the terms of the leases. The court finds no cause." He set the pages aside, squared their edges, and reached for the next matter on the list. A low sound moved through the back benches, not quite a groan. Francis did not sit down. He waited, and when his father still did not look at him, he gathered the ledgers into his arms and spoke to the top of the bowed grey head. "Then I'll take it to the county assizes," he said, "and I'll put these records before a judge who reads them." The clerk stopped writing. Benedict turned a page of the list and called the next name.

Clara Ashby was waiting at the foot of the stairs, her shawl pulled close against the draught from the street door. She had kept the eviction records herself, two winters of them, in her father's careful columns and then in her own when his hands grew too stiff to hold a pen: every family's name, the rent asked, the date the bailiffs came, where the children slept after. Francis put the ledgers back into her keeping, and she held them against her chest as though they might be snatched. "They'll want someone to swear to them at the assizes," he said. "Someone who wrote them." Clara looked past him, up the stairwell toward the courtroom door, and then back at him. "I'll swear to every line," she said.

Outside, the market had begun to pack up, and a cold light lay across the square. On the courthouse steps Francis stopped, one stair below her, so that for once they stood eye to eye. He spoke quietly, without any of the polish he had used before the bench, and asked her to marry him. Clara gave a short laugh of pure surprise, then pressed her lips together and studied his face for a long moment, the ledgers still clasped in her arms. "Yes," she said. Behind them the court door opened and shut as the weavers came down in twos and threes, and a few of them slowed on the steps, caps in hand, to watch.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="f4cad600-0fca-4584-9b00-765f2c784d22">Francis took Clara's eviction records to the council chamber at Harrowgate.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1415298e-86c4-4510-af06-d945190e9400" -->

The study at Hallowmere smelled of beeswax and coal smoke, and of the ledgers that lined the east wall in their calfskin rows, each spine stamped in gilt with a year and the name of the mill. Benedict Tomson sat behind the desk with his back to the window, so that the grey afternoon fell over his shoulders and left his face in shadow. He did not rise when his son came in. Francis still wore his travelling coat, the hem dark with road mud, and he held his hat in both hands as though he had forgotten he carried it.

"The jury found for the weavers," Francis said. "Unanimously, and within the hour. Judge Halloran called the wage deductions unlawful from the bench. He said the argument was as clear as any he had heard on the circuit." He paused, and when his father said nothing, he went on more quickly. "I came straight here. I wanted you to hear it from me, and I wanted to ask you something while I stood in front of you. I mean to marry Clara Ashby. I have asked her, and she has said yes. I should like your blessing, sir."

Benedict set down his pen and squared it against the edge of the blotter. "You stood up in the Shire Hall," he said, "before half the magistrates in the county, and you told them that the Tomson mill had been robbing its own hands. You put my name in the mouths of every alehouse between here and Leeds. And you come to me expecting congratulation." He looked at his son for the first time. "That was not a victory, Francis. That was a humiliation, and you were the instrument of it."

"The deductions were wrong, Father. You know they were."

"What I know," Benedict said, "is that a man's son does not choose the side against him. And now you mean to bind yourself to a weaver's daughter, the girl whose father stood at the front of that rabble and named me a thief. Have you any notion how that will be spoken of? Every one of them will believe the Tomsons can be bought by the cottage door." He drew a ledger toward him and opened it without looking down. "Marry her if you must. You are of age, and I cannot stop you. But I will not sit in a church and watch it done. I will not attend."

Francis stood a moment longer while the clock on the mantel counted off the silence. The coals shifted in the grate. His father had already lifted his pen again and was running it down a column of figures, his lips moving faintly as he totted them, and he did not raise his eyes when his son turned his hat once in his hands, set it on his head, and went out, leaving the door open behind him to the cold of the passage.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="6778afab-8b6d-4a23-9954-9eb738d02011">Francis left Hallowmere and went down to Mill Lane, to sit up through the night beside a fevered weaver.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="db172074-3157-4555-90e0-c48dca73fcd8" -->

The letter came at dusk, carried up the lane by the carrier's boy, who would not stay for a cup of cider. Francis knew the hand before he broke the seal: the hard downstrokes, the capitals pressed so deep the pen had nearly torn the paper. He read it standing by the window of the Ashbys' small front room, where the last light fell grey across the flagstones. It ran to four lines. *I have told you already that I will not attend. I see no cause to alter what I have said. Do not write to ask it again. B. Whitlock.* There was no greeting and no blessing, and there was no word of Clara at all.

He folded it once, then again, until it was a hard little square in his palm. Clara sat by the hearth with her mother's lace across her knees, mending a place where the moth had been, and she watched him without speaking. When at last he turned, his face had the look of a man working through sums he did not like. "Clara," he said, and then stopped, and began again more quietly. "If we were to wait. A month only, or until the spring. He has changed his mind before. If he had time to think, if he saw that I meant to stand by it—" He could not finish. Clara laid the lace aside, and her hands were very still in her lap.

It was her father who answered. Thomas Ashby had been sitting in the corner with his pipe gone cold, and he rose now with the stiffness of a man who had spent forty years bent over a loom. "You'd wait on a man who won't come to his own son's wedding," he said, "for him to tell you that what you did was right?" He crossed to the window and pointed down the lane, where lamps were being lit one by one in the row of cottages: the Pratts', the Dunnings', old Hester Lane's, where the widow sat at her frame again with her grandchildren asleep around her. "Them as were turned out are back under their own roofs. That's the verdict on your work, lad, and it's the only one worth the having. Your father may sit in his great house and think what he likes. There's forty families down there who know what you are."

Francis looked a long while at the lit windows. Then he opened his hand and put the folded letter on the mantel, square and small beside the candlestick, and he did not pick it up again. He went to Clara and knelt by her chair, and she put her hand on his hair. "Tomorrow," he said. "At ten, as we settled it. I'll not ask you to wait on him." Thomas Ashby grunted, found his tinderbox, and set about lighting his pipe again, and the smoke curled up blue toward the low black beams while outside the lane went on filling with lamplight.

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="81a89b65-7856-4455-a934-2c8a3a60084a">Francis went to the chapel at Ashbury on the wedding morning, with the front pew still kept empty for his father.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="4b9644b9-a9b1-4b36-ab5d-7f9893291807" -->

The chapel doors swung outward on their iron hinges, and the noise of the village came in at once: bells overhead, a dog barking somewhere past the green, the scrape of boots on the worn stone of the steps. Francis came out into the cold bright morning with Clara Ashby's hand in the crook of his arm. She wore a dress of her own weaving, grey-blue and plain at the hem, and there was still a thread of wool caught in her hair that no one had thought to pull loose. The people of Ashbury stood crowded below the steps, the weavers at the front, their caps in their hands and their faces raw from the wind. Behind them, along the lane that ran down toward the river, the cottages stood with their shutters thrown open and smoke rising from every chimney.

Someone began to cheer, and the rest took it up, unevenly at first and then all together. Old Tobias Reed, who had kept his loom in the same front room for forty years, lifted his stick over his head and shook it. Clara laughed and pressed closer to Francis's side. He smiled down at the crowd, but at the top of the steps he turned his head and looked back through the open doors into the dim length of the nave.

The front pew on the left side, the one with the carved oak end and the faded crest, sat empty. A hymnal lay closed on the cushion where it had been set out that morning by the sexton, squared neatly to the edge of the seat, untouched. The light from the east window fell across it in long coloured bars. Francis looked at it for the space of a breath, then another, as though the pew might yet fill if he gave it time. The organ had gone quiet. The vicar, waiting in the aisle with his book against his chest, followed his gaze and then looked away.

Clara said nothing. She only waited, her hand steady on his arm.

Francis drew in the cold air and let it go. Something in his shoulders loosened, as a man's do when he sets down a weight he has carried so long he has forgotten it was there. He turned his back on the doors and on the empty place behind them, and he did not look round again. He took Clara's hand properly in his own, lacing his fingers through hers, and led her down the steps into the noise and the crowd, where the weavers closed around them and the bells went on ringing over the roofs of Ashbury.

